  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Suppose $p\\ne q$. Find the (single) point of intersection of the graphs of $y=(x-p)^2$ and $y=(x-q)^2$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\left(\\dfrac{p+q}{2},\\dfrac{(p-q)^2}{4}\\right)$.\n\nEquate the two expressions for $y$, solve for $x$, then substitute.",
      "worked": "At the intersection,\n$$\\begin{align*}\n(x-p)^2&=(x-q)^2\\\\\nx^2-2px+p^2&=x^2-2qx+q^2\\\\\n2(q-p)x&=q^2-p^2\\\\\n2(q-p)x&=(q-p)(q+p).\n\\end{align*}$$\nSince $p\\ne q$, division by $q-p$ gives\n$$x=\\frac{p+q}{2}.$$\nSubstituting into $y=(x-p)^2$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\ny&=\\left(\\frac{p+q}{2}-p\\right)^2\\\\\ny&=\\left(\\frac{q-p}{2}\\right)^2\\\\\ny&=\\frac{(p-q)^2}{4}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThere is exactly one solution for $x$, so the single point of intersection is $\\left(\\dfrac{p+q}{2},\\dfrac{(p-q)^2}{4}\\right)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 80
  },
